# Team Oreo backend

Express 5, MongoDB/Mongoose, Argon2id, JWT cookie authentication and Cloudinary.
The agreed API includes public articles/contact forms and protected administration for
articles, enquiries, uploads and the dashboard. There is no public registration.

## Setup

Use Node.js 22.21.1 or newer (the dependency floor is Node.js 20.19).

```bash
npm ci
cp .env.example .env
# Fill in local/staging credentials and a random JWT_SECRET before starting.
npm run seed:admin
npm run dev
# Production process:
npm start
```

`npm run dev` uses Node's watch mode. `npm start` validates configuration, connects
to MongoDB, waits for model indexes and then listens. A duplicate legacy slug/email
can prevent the integrity index from being created; resolve such data in staging
before deployment. Startup does not drop indexes or reset data.
`GET /api/health` returns `{"success":true,"message":"TeamOreo API is running"}`.
The server listens only after MongoDB is available; the health endpoint is a process
health check, not an ongoing dependency readiness probe.

## Environment

Never commit `.env` or credentials. `.env.example` contains configuration names and
safe examples. The application does not automatically provision admins at startup.

| Variable | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `PORT` | TCP port, default `5000` |
| `NODE_ENV` | Set `production` for HTTPS Secure cookies |
| `MONGODB_URI` | Required MongoDB URI; use a dedicated staging/test database for verification |
| `JWT_SECRET` | Required random secret, at least 32 characters |
| `JWT_EXPIRES_IN` | Default `2h`; positive integer seconds or integer with `s`, `m`, `h`, `d`, `w`; maximum 24 days |
| `CLIENT_ORIGIN` | Required comma-separated exact HTTP(S) frontend origins, without paths, wildcards or trailing slashes; HTTPS required in production |
| `COOKIE_SAME_SITE` | `lax` (default), `strict`, or `none`; `none` requires production HTTPS Secure cookies |
| `TRUST_PROXY` | Optional explicit trusted proxy hop count; leave unset for a directly exposed server |
| `ADMIN_NAME` | Name used by the admin provisioning script |
| `ADMIN_EMAIL` | Email used by the admin provisioning script |
| `ADMIN_PASSWORD` | Password used by the admin provisioning script, 12-200 characters |
| `CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME` | Cloudinary cloud/account name; required for uploads and managed cleanup |
| `CLOUDINARY_API_KEY` | Cloudinary server API key |
| `CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET` | Cloudinary server API secret |

Cloudinary credentials are optional for running article/contact CRUD with external
image URLs. Missing/invalid image-service credentials produce `502 CLOUDINARY_ERROR`
on upload, with safe client output. Cleanup failures retain the remote image and do
not roll back a successful database change.

Generate a secret locally:

```bash
node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('hex'))"
```

Set only the trusted number of proxy hops. A permissive proxy setting can let clients
spoof the IP used by rate limiting. Rate limits use process-local memory: use one API
instance for the documented quotas, or configure a shared store before scaling out.

## Admin provisioning and authentication

Set `ADMIN_NAME`, `ADMIN_EMAIL`, `ADMIN_PASSWORD` and `MONGODB_URI` in `.env`, then run
`npm run seed:admin`. This creates an admin only if that email does not already exist.
It does not reset an existing password.

For an intentional create/reset, use the CLI (be aware command arguments may enter
shell history; prefer environment-based provisioning for the initial account):

```bash
node src/scripts/createAdmin.js admin@example.com 'a-strong-example-password' 'Oreo Admin'
```

Omitting the optional CLI name preserves an existing account name.
Both scripts validate inputs and hash passwords with Argon2id. Legacy bcrypt hashes
remain supported to preserve existing accounts; successful login upgrades them to
Argon2id. New accounts never use bcrypt.

`POST /api/admin/auth/login` accepts only `{ "email": "admin@example.com", "password": "..." }`.
The email is trimmed and lowercased; the password is never trimmed.
Success (`200`) sets `admin_token` and returns:

```json
{
  "success": true,
  "data": { "admin": { "id": "mongo-object-id", "name": "Oreo Admin", "email": "admin@example.com" } },
  "message": "Logged in"
}
```

`GET /api/admin/auth/me` returns the same `data.admin` structure without the message.
It verifies an HS256 JWT and loads the current admin; deleted admins cannot restore a
session. Password hashes and tokens are never returned in JSON.
Invalid credentials return `401 INVALID_CREDENTIALS` with a generic message.
Login permits 10 attempts per IP per 15 minutes, including validation failures.

`POST /api/admin/auth/logout` requires an authenticated session and clears the cookie
using matching attributes. Expired sessions already receive `401` and can be treated
as logged out by the frontend. JWTs are stateless: clearing the browser cookie does
not revoke an independently copied token. Rotate the signing secret to invalidate
all sessions when required.

## CORS, cookies and frontend integration

For local development:

```dotenv
CLIENT_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3002
NODE_ENV=development
COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax
PORT=5000
```

Use `localhost` consistently for both frontend and backend (mixing `127.0.0.1` and
`localhost` changes cookie/site behavior). Send credentials on login and every
admin request:

```javascript
const response = await fetch('http://localhost:5000/api/admin/auth/login', {
  method: 'POST',
  credentials: 'include',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ email, password }),
});
const result = await response.json();
// Session restoration:
await fetch('http://localhost:5000/api/admin/auth/me', { credentials: 'include' });
```

For related production sites, for example `https://www.example.com` and
`https://api.example.com`, use `NODE_ENV=production`, `COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax` and the
exact frontend origin(s). For unrelated production sites use `COOKIE_SAME_SITE=none`;
HTTPS is mandatory. Browser third-party-cookie restrictions can still prevent
sessions: a same-site API domain or same-origin reverse proxy avoids that dependency.
Cookies are HttpOnly, host-only, path `/`; their Max-Age uses the same integer lifetime
as the JWT. Logout clears those same attributes.

Multiple frontend origins:

```dotenv
CLIENT_ORIGIN=https://www.example.com,https://admin.example.com
```

CORS echoes only allowlisted origins and enables credentials; preflight is supported.
Origin protection uses the same list for writes. Non-browser CLI/server requests
without Origin are intentionally allowed; explicit cross-site browser writes without
Origin are rejected. Send JSON for API bodies; only uploads accept multipart. CORS
is not authentication: every protected route independently checks the admin cookie.

## Response and error contract

Success: `{ "success": true, "data": ..., "message": "optional message" }`.
Health omits `data`. List responses use:

```json
{ "success": true, "data": { "items": [], "page": 1, "limit": 9, "total": 0, "totalPages": 1 } }
```

Error: `{ "success": false, "error": { "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR", "message": "Check the submitted fields", "fields": { "title": "Invalid value" } } }`.
The optional fields map identifies invalid fields. Do not depend on specific validator
message text; use the status, error code and field names. A single final error handler
maps parser, Mongoose, multipart, Cloudinary and unexpected errors. Unexpected errors
return safe messages, without stack traces or upstream secrets.

| Status | Codes |
| --- | --- |
| 400 | `VALIDATION_ERROR`, `INVALID_JSON`, `INVALID_FILE` |
| 401 | `INVALID_CREDENTIALS`, `UNAUTHENTICATED` |
| 403 | `FORBIDDEN_ORIGIN` |
| 404 | `NOT_FOUND` |
| 409 | `CONFLICT` (unique value or concurrent article edit) |
| 413 | `FILE_TOO_LARGE`, `PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE` |
| 429 | `RATE_LIMITED` |
| 500 | `INTERNAL_ERROR` |
| 502 | `CLOUDINARY_ERROR` |

JSON bodies are limited to 1 MB. Malformed IDs return 400; valid IDs with no matching
record return 404. Unknown routes return a JSON 404. Unsupported mutation fields,
empty PATCH bodies, and incorrect types are rejected. Query values must be scalar;
repeated page/search/category/status parameters are invalid. Page is an integer
1-1,000,000 and limit is 1-50. Search is literal case-insensitive text, up to 200
characters (regular-expression operators are escaped).

## Complete API inventory

All `/api/admin/*` routes require the admin cookie except login.

| Method | Endpoint | Contract |
| --- | --- | --- |
| GET | `/api/health` | Process health |
| POST | `/api/admin/auth/login` | JSON email/password; set session cookie |
| GET | `/api/admin/auth/me` | Restore `data.admin` |
| POST | `/api/admin/auth/logout` | Clear cookie; `data: null` |
| GET | `/api/blogs` | Published cards; `page`, `limit` (default 9), `category`, `search` |
| GET | `/api/blogs/:slug` | Full published article; draft/missing gives 404 |
| GET | `/api/admin/blogs` | All articles; same filters plus `status=draft|published` |
| GET | `/api/admin/blogs/:id` | Full article for editor |
| POST | `/api/admin/blogs` | Create article; 201 and `data` article |
| PATCH | `/api/admin/blogs/:id` | Edit/publish/unpublish; 200 and `data` article |
| DELETE | `/api/admin/blogs/:id` | Hard delete; 200, `data: null` |
| POST | `/api/admin/uploads` | Multipart `image`; 201 and image metadata |
| POST | `/api/contact` | Submit enquiry; 201, `data: null` |
| GET | `/api/admin/contacts` | List enquiries; `page`, `limit` (default 10), `status`, `search` |
| GET | `/api/admin/contacts/:id` | Full enquiry; does not implicitly mark it read |
| PATCH | `/api/admin/contacts/:id` | JSON `{ "status": "new|read|resolved" }` |
| DELETE | `/api/admin/contacts/:id` | Hard delete; 200, `data: null` |
| GET | `/api/admin/dashboard/summary` | Counts and up to five recent enquiries |

## Article workflow and fields

Example create body:

```json
{
  "title": "Team Oreo News",
  "excerpt": "News from the Team Oreo community.",
  "content": "<h2>Match day</h2><p>The full article.</p>",
  "featuredImage": { "url": "https://example.com/photo.jpg", "alt": "Team photo" },
  "category": "News",
  "author": "Oreo Admin",
  "status": "draft",
  "seoTitle": "Team Oreo News",
  "seoDescription": "Latest community news."
}
```

Required: title 3-200 characters, excerpt 10-500, content 1-500,000, category 1-100,
author 1-150 and featuredImage.url (HTTP(S), up to 2048). Optional: slug (1-220 input
characters, normalized for URLs), status (defaults to draft), image alt/publicId
(up to 300 each), seoTitle (up to 200), seoDescription (up to 500). Whitespace is
trimmed from text fields. HTML is sanitized before persistence; script-only content
that becomes empty is rejected. Scripts, handlers, unsafe protocols and embedded
frames are not retained.

To publish: `PATCH { "status": "published" }` sets publishedAt if absent. To unpublish:
`PATCH { "status": "draft" }` clears publishedAt. Republishing sets a new timestamp.
Changing a title preserves the existing URL. An explicit slug change is normalized;
resubmitting an effectively identical slug preserves it. Collisions normally get
numeric suffixes. A concurrent unique-index collision retries with a random suffix
(up to four retries), then returns 409. The database unique slug index remains the
integrity guarantee. Optimistic concurrency prevents stale simultaneous updates
from overwriting each other; reload on 409.

Public listings exclude article content and drafts, sort newest published first,
and support exact category filtering and title/excerpt search. Public slug detail
also requires published status. Admin list/detail includes content and all statuses.
Articles carry `_id`, slug, publishedAt, createdAt, updatedAt and the documented fields.
On PATCH, featuredImage replaces the entire image object; always include its URL and
preserve publicId/alt when keeping the existing image.

## Uploads and image lifecycle

```javascript
const form = new FormData();
form.append('image', file);
const response = await fetch(`${apiBase}/api/admin/uploads`, {
  method: 'POST', credentials: 'include', body: form,
});
// Let the browser set the multipart Content-Type/boundary.
```

One JPEG, PNG or WebP, up to 5 MB. MIME and basic byte signatures are checked locally;
Cloudinary validates the image and allowed formats. Missing files give 400, invalid
MIME/signature/field gives `400 INVALID_FILE`, oversize gives `413 FILE_TOO_LARGE`.
Success:

```json
{
  "success": true,
  "data": { "url": "https://res.cloudinary.com/cloud/image/upload/v123/team-oreo/blogs/asset.png", "publicId": "team-oreo/blogs/asset", "bytes": 102400 },
  "message": "Image uploaded"
}
```

Save both `url` and `publicId` into the article's featuredImage; add alt text in the
editor. Cleanup runs after a successful article deletion or image replacement.
Only canonical HTTPS URLs in the configured Cloudinary account's `team-oreo/blogs`
folder with a matching stored publicId are managed. URL-only legacy records,
external URLs, transformed/noncanonical URLs and other folders/accounts are retained;
no identifier is guessed from a legacy URL. Assets still referenced by another
article (by publicId or exact URL) are retained.

Remote deletion uses invalidation. A cleanup/database-reference-check failure logs
the managed publicId for an operator to retry and preserves the successful article
write. There is no background retry queue. An upload abandoned before saving an
article, or a retained legacy asset, requires deliberate manual reconciliation in
Cloudinary. Avoid reusing an asset across articles during concurrent deletion; the
reference check is best effort across independent database/Cloudinary systems.

## Contact workflow and dashboard

Contact submission requires name (2-100), email (valid, up to 254) and message
(10-5000). Optional established fields: companyName (up to 150), phone (5-30 digits,
spaces and `+()-`), subject (up to 150). There is no attachment API. Empty optional
fields may be omitted. Status and timestamps are server-owned. `website` is the
honeypot: leave it empty; filled values receive an acknowledgement with no record.
Public responses never expose enquiry data. Five successful submissions per IP per
hour are allowed; failed requests do not consume the quota.

Admin search covers name, email, companyName and subject. Lists omit message and
phone; detail returns the complete enquiry. Set status explicitly to `new`, `read`
or `resolved`. Deletion is permanent.

Dashboard `data` is `{ totalArticles, publishedArticles, totalEnquiries, newEnquiries,
recentEnquiries }`; recentEnquiries is at most five newest records with `_id`, name,
email, subject, status and createdAt. Zero records produce zero counts and an empty
array. No unrelated analytics are added.

## Verification and deployment

```bash
npm test
npm audit --omit=dev
npm run test:coverage
git diff --check
```

The original five app-level tests are retained. Expanded HTTP tests use real Express,
Mongoose document validation, JWT, Argon2id/bcrypt, multipart parsing and sanitization,
with isolated in-memory model query/persistence doubles, mocked provisioning
connections, and a Cloudinary stream
double. They open only an ephemeral loopback HTTP server. No production database or
Cloudinary asset is used or deleted. Coverage is a local code metric, not proof of
external service behavior.

Before release, use a disposable/staging database and Cloudinary account to verify:
provision admin; login and cookie restoration in the actual browser/origin topology;
article CRUD and draft/publish/unpublish; literal search and pagination; contact
submission/status/deletion; dashboard; upload a disposable image and confirm
replacement/deletion cleanup; logout. Also test the deployed proxy IP/rate-limit
configuration. Never use production records for destructive verification. Ensure
HTTPS, MongoDB backups, restricted database credentials/network access and a process
supervisor are configured by the hosting environment.
